Package: rhodep-gnss
Version: 5
Architecture: arm64
Depends: python3, python3-gi
Recommends: gpsd, gpsd-clients, geoclue-2.0, network-manager, gir1.2-qmi-1.0, libqmi-proxy
Suggests: iw, libqmi-utils
Maintainer: rhodep port
Description: WiFi, cell-tower and cell-id location as NMEA for Motorola rhodep
 Gives this phone a working location fix without satellite GNSS, which is
 unusable here: starting the measurement engine power-cycles the SoC inside
 100 ms, reproducibly. Publishes whatever position it can get as ordinary
 NMEA on a UNIX socket (/run/gnss-share.sock) and on TCP 127.0.0.1:2948, so
 gpsd and geoclue can consume it, and ships drop-ins pointing both at it.
 .
 Three sources, selected with --source (default 'auto'):
 .
  * wifi - scans the WiFi neighbourhood and asks an Ichnaea-compatible
    geolocation service where those BSSIDs are. Needs no SIM, no modem and no
    GNSS engine, so it cannot trip the reset bug even in principle. Queries
    BeaconDB first and falls back to Positon, because BeaconDB is young and
    has no coverage in much of the world. Access points whose SSID ends in
    _nomap are excluded, and nothing is ever submitted back. MEASURED in
    Rosario/AR: 22 m.
  * cell - reads which GERAN/UMTS/LTE cells the modem can hear, via the
    read-only QMI NAS message get-cell-location-info, and resolves those
    identities against a local OpenCelliD extract and then the same
    geolocation services. This works with an unprovisioned SIM and a modem
    that never registers, because a modem decodes the broadcast identity of
    the cells around it while it is still searching for a network -- which is
    also why it is intermittent, so the reader polls, caches and reports
    staleness. MEASURED in Rosario/AR: 250 m from Positon.
  * qmi-cellid - a QMI LOC session pinned to operation mode 'cellid', which
    only yields a position once the modem has a serving cell it can resolve
    itself; in practice that needs a provisioned SIM.
 .
 'auto' reads modem registration state from ModemManager over D-Bus - a plain
 property read that goes nowhere near the location engine - and uses
 qmi-cellid only while the modem is actually registered, otherwise wifi and
 cell together, preferring wifi. The QMI LOC service is never opened unless
 that registration check passes, which puts the entire class of GNSS reset
 bugs out of reach rather than merely guarding against it. The cell source
 allocates a QMI NAS client, which is a different service and cannot start the
 measurement engine.
 .
 When the qmi-cellid path is used it is pinned to 'cellid' and the daemon
 refuses to start a session unless the modem confirms that mode three times -
 in the set-operation-mode ack, in its indication, and in a get-operation-mode
 readback. This is not belt and braces: a set_operation_mode that is merely
 acked but never applied leaves the engine in standalone, which reboots the
 phone. The three verdicts are recorded and all three are re-asserted at the
 single point where QMI_LOC_START is sent, because this modem delivers the
 indication before the ack and a chain would otherwise pass the second gate
 before the first had answered. See docs/interconnect-sm6375-wip/GNSS-SM6375.md.
 .
 ModemManager is the other way into the same measurement engine, and it has no
 such guard: its Location interface advertises gps-raw, gps-nmea, agps-msa and
 agps-msb on this modem, any of which resets the phone and any of which is one
 unprivileged D-Bus call away. This package therefore ships a mandatory D-Bus
 deny for Location.Setup, Location.InjectAssistanceData and
 Location.SetSuplServer, and a rhodep-qmi-proxy.service so that restarting the
 location daemon no longer kills the qmi-proxy ModemManager is talking through
 - which used to re-enumerate the modem and make ModemManager re-probe the LOC
 service for SUPL and XTRA assistance data on every restart.
 .
 Positions are published as synthesized $GPGGA/$GPRMC/$GPGSA at 1 Hz, plus
 $GPGST carrying the real accuracy in metres. IP-address and cell-area
 fallback answers from the geolocation services are refused by default: they
 are tens of kilometres wide and publishing one as a GPS fix would be
 undetectable downstream. So is any fix too coarse to state honestly in
 $GPGGA: geoclue derives accuracy from HDOP and its coarsest bucket means
 300 m, so a 1200 m cell fix would reach it as a claim of 300 m that nothing
 in the stream could contradict. See --max-nmea-accuracy.
 .
 geoclue's own [wifi] source is turned off by the shipped configuration for
 the same reason. It cannot send "considerIp": false, so when a service
 cannot match the BSSIDs it answers with the position of the ISP, and geoclue
 passes that on: measured here as a 26 km "fix" 4 km from the phone, which
 beat the 22 m one this daemon was producing at the same moment. Turning it
 off also stops a second copy of the BSSID list leaving the phone every
 minute. Note that clients must ask geoclue for EXACT accuracy to get this
 daemon's position at all, because geoclue will not start a source more
 accurate than the client requested.
 .
 rhodep-cell-db builds the offline database from the OpenCelliD or Mozilla
 Location Service dumps. Its systemd timer is shipped but NOT enabled: a
 refresh streams over a gigabyte.
 .
 rhodep-xtra is the gpsOneXTRA path this port was missing: the job stock
 Android gives to xtra-daemon. It fetches Qualcomm's predicted-orbit almanac
 from the three CDN endpoints the modem names itself and injects it over QMI
 LOC, together with UTC time and a coarse position taken from the wifi source.
 Without it the modem's almanac reads 1980-01-06T00:00:00Z valid for 0 hours,
 the GPS epoch, meaning it has never been loaded. Its timer IS enabled: it is
 40 kB every six hours, and unlike anything that touches the measurement
 engine it cannot trip the reset. Injection is what A-GPS does before a fix;
 the only message that starts the engine is QMI_LOC_START, which this tool
 refuses to send through an allow-list asserted on every QMI write.
 .
 `rhodep-xtra status` is also the port's only read-only inventory of stored LOC
 state, including the SUPL server, which qmicli cannot show.
